# vhost-user Back Ends

vhost-user back ends service VirtIO device requests outside of QEMU itself.

## vhost-user Device

Simple stub devices ensuring the VirtIO device is visible to the guest. Each device has a `chardev` option connecting via socket to the vhost-user daemon.

### Available Devices

| Device | Type | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| vhost-user-blk | Block storage | storage-daemon |
| vhost-user-fs | File storage | virtiofsd |
| vhost-user-gpu | GPU | vhost-device-gpu |
| vhost-user-input | Input | vhost-device-input |
| vhost-user-rng | Entropy | vhost-device-rng |
| vhost-user-snd | Audio | vhost-device-sound |
| vhost-user-scsi | SCSI storage | vhost_user_scsi |
| vhost-user-vsock | Socket comm | vhost-device-vsock |

### vhost-user-test-device

Generic development device for expert use. Parameters: `virtio-id`, `num_vqs`, `vq_size`, `config_size`.

## Shared Memory Object

The daemon needs guest address space access via `memory-backend-file`, `memory-backend-memfd`, or `memory-backend-shm` objects. Must match the size of main system memory.

## Example

```
$ virtio-foo --socket-path=/var/run/foo.sock $OTHER_ARGS

$ qemu-system-x86_64 \
    -m 4096 \
    -chardev socket,id=ba1,path=/var/run/foo.sock \
    -device vhost-user-foo,chardev=ba1,$OTHER_ARGS \
    -object memory-backend-memfd,id=mem,size=4G,share=on \
    -numa node,memdev=mem
```
