# vhost-user Daemons in Contrib

QEMU provides a number of vhost-user daemons in the contrib directory. You should also consider if other vhost-user daemons such as those from the rust-vmm [vhost-device repository](https://github.com/rust-vmm/vhost-device) are better suited for production use.

## vhost-user-block - Block Device

A backend for exposing block devices. You almost certainly want to use the storage-daemon instead which supports a wide variety of storage modes.

## vhost-user-gpu - GPU Device

Presents a paravirtualized GPU and display controller. You probably want to use the internal virtio-gpu implementation for latest features.

## vhost-user-input - Input Emulation

The vhost-user-input device implementation polls on input devices and passes input events to the guest.

Requires guest Linux kernel built with `CONFIG_VIRTIO_INPUT`.

Example:
```
host# vhost-user-input --socket-path=input.sock --evdev-path=/dev/input/event17

host# qemu-system-x86_64 \
    -chardev socket,path=/tmp/input.sock,id=mouse0 \
    -device vhost-user-input-pci,chardev=mouse0 \
    -m 4096 \
    -object memory-backend-file,id=mem,size=4G,mem-path=/dev/shm,share=on \
    -numa node,memdev=mem
```

## vhost-user-scsi - SCSI Controller

The vhost-user-scsi daemon can proxy iSCSI devices onto a virtualized SCSI controller.

## vhost-user-bridge - Network Bridge

A development tool for testing real internet traffic by providing a networking backend for the vhost-user protocol.

Example:
```
$ vhost-user-bridge -u /tmp/vubr.sock -l 127.0.0.1:4444 -r 127.0.0.1:5555

$ qemu-system-x86_64 \
    -m 4G \
    -object memory-backend-memfd,id=mem0,size=4G,share=on,prealloc=on \
    -numa node,memdev=mem0 \
    -chardev socket,id=char0,path=/tmp/vubr.sock \
    -netdev vhost-user,id=vhost0,chardev=char0,vhostforce=on \
    -device virtio-net-pci,netdev=vhost0
```
