title: "AMD Secure Encrypted Virtualization (SEV)"
source: "system/i386/amd-memory-encryption.rst"
---

# AMD Secure Encrypted Virtualization (SEV)

SEV is an extension to the AMD-V architecture which supports running encrypted VMs under the control of KVM. Each encrypted VM is associated with a unique encryption key.

SEV-ES builds on SEV to additionally protect the guest register state.

## Launching (SEV and SEV-ES)

Boot images must be encrypted before a guest can be booted. The `MEMORY_ENCRYPT_OP` ioctl provides commands: `LAUNCH_START`, `LAUNCH_UPDATE_DATA`, `LAUNCH_MEASURE`, `LAUNCH_FINISH`.

### Guest Policy

```
sev-guest,id=sev0,policy=0x1
```

Setting bit 2 launches as SEV-ES guest:
```
sev-guest,id=sev0,policy=0x5
```

### Launch Commands

- `LAUNCH_START` - Create cryptographic launch context
- `LAUNCH_UPDATE_DATA` - Encrypt memory region
- `LAUNCH_UPDATE_VMSA` - Encrypt vCPU VMSAs (SEV-ES only)
- `LAUNCH_MEASURE` - Retrieve measurement for attestation
- `LAUNCH_FINISH` - Finalize guest launch

### Launching Examples

SEV guest:
```
-machine ...,confidential-guest-support=sev0
-object sev-guest,id=sev0,cbitpos=47,reduced-phys-bits=1
```

SEV-ES guest:
```
-machine ...,confidential-guest-support=sev0
-object sev-guest,id=sev0,cbitpos=47,reduced-phys-bits=1,policy=0x5
```

### SEV-ES Restrictions

- Does not support SMM
- Does not support reboot
- Requires in-kernel irqchip

## Launching (SEV-SNP)

Commands: `SNP_LAUNCH_START`, `SNP_LAUNCH_UPDATE`, `SNP_LAUNCH_FINISH`.

```
-machine ...,confidential-guest-support=sev0
-object sev-snp-guest,id=sev0,cbitpos=51,reduced-phys-bits=1
```

## References

- [AMD Memory Encryption whitepaper](https://www.amd.com/content/dam/amd/en/documents/epyc-business-docs/white-papers/memory-encryption-white-paper.pdf)
- [SEV API Spec](https://www.amd.com/system/files/TechDocs/55766_SEV-KM_API_Specification.pdf)
ENDOFFILE
