# Hyper-V Enlightenments

KVM on x86 implements Hyper-V Enlightenments for Windows guests. These features make Windows and Hyper-V guests think they're running on top of a Hyper-V compatible hypervisor.

## Setup

No Hyper-V enlightenments are enabled by default. Enable through CPU flags:

```
qemu-system-x86_64 --enable-kvm --cpu host,hv_relaxed,hv_vpindex,hv_time,...
```

## Existing Enlightenments

| Feature | Description |
|---|---|
| `hv-relaxed` | Disable watchdog timeouts |
| `hv-vapic` | VP Assist page MSR for efficient APIC access |
| `hv-spinlocks=xxx` | Paravirtualized spinlocks |
| `hv-vpindex` | Virtual processor index MSR |
| `hv-runtime` | Virtual processor run time MSR |
| `hv-crash` | Crash information MSRs |
| `hv-time` | Hyper-V clocksources (MSR-based + Reference TSC page) |
| `hv-synic` | Synthetic interrupt controller (requires hv-vpindex) |
| `hv-stimer` | Synthetic timers (requires hv-vpindex, hv-synic, hv-time) |
| `hv-tlbflush` | Paravirtualized TLB shoot-down (requires hv-vpindex) |
| `hv-ipi` | Paravirtualized IPI (requires hv-vpindex) |
| `hv-vendor-id=xxx` | Change Hyper-V identification |
| `hv-reset` | Guest self-reset MSR |
| `hv-frequencies` | TSC/APIC frequency MSRs |
| `hv-reenlightenment` | TSC frequency change notification (nested) |
| `hv-evmcs` | Enlightened VMCS v1 (Intel only, nested) |
| `hv-stimer-direct` | Direct mode synthetic timers |
| `hv-avic` | Hyper-V SynIC with hardware APICv/AVIC |
| `hv-no-nonarch-coresharing` | SMT coresharing control |
| `hv-syndbg` | Synthetic debugger interface |
| `hv-emsr-bitmap` | Enlightened MSR-Bitmap (nested) |
| `hv-xmm-input` | XMM Fast Hypercall Input |
| `hv-tlbflush-ext` | Extended GVA ranges for TLB flush |
| `hv-tlbflush-direct` | Direct TLB flush (nested) |

## Supplementary Features

- `hv-passthrough` - Give Windows all supported enlightenments
- `hv-enforce-cpuid` - Limit to exposed features only

## Recommendations

Enable all currently implemented enlightenments with these exceptions:
- `hv-syndbg`, `hv-passthrough`, `hv-enforce-cpuid` should not be in production
- `hv-evmcs` - Intel CPUs only
- `hv-no-nonarch-coresharing` - only with proper vCPU pinning
- `hv-crash` - only if crash info consumed via QAPI
- `hv-spinlocks` - set to 0xfff when CPUs are overcommitted
ENDOFFILE
