# Security

## Security Requirements

### Virtualization Use Case

Covers cloud/VPS hosting, data center and desktop virtualization. Untrusted entities:

- Guest
- User-facing interfaces (VNC, SPICE, WebSocket)
- Network protocols (NBD, live migration)
- User-supplied files (disk images, kernels)
- Passthrough devices (PCI, USB)

Supported machine types: `virt` (aarch64, riscv, loongarch), `pc`/`q35`/`microvm` (x86), `s390-ccw-virtio`, `pseries` (ppc64).

### Non-virtualization Use Case

TCG emulation. Bugs are not considered security bugs. No guest isolation guarantees.

## Architecture

### Guest Isolation

Guest code must be confined to the virtual machine. Bugs in emulated devices could allow guest escape.

### Principle of Least Privilege

QEMU process should only have access to resources belonging to the guest.

### Isolation Mechanisms

- Run QEMU as unprivileged user
- SELinux / AppArmor
- Resource limits and cgroups
- Linux namespaces
- seccomp (`--sandbox` option)

## Sensitive Configurations

### Monitor Console (QMP/HMP)

- Should be exposed over UNIX domain socket only
- TCP requires TLS encryption and authorization
- Equivalent to privileges of the QEMU user account
