title: "Paravirtualized KVM Features"
source: "system/i386/kvm-pv.rst"
---

# Paravirtualized KVM Features

KVM implements its own paravirtualized interfaces when implementing hardware interfaces in software is slow.

## Default Features

The following features are enabled by default when KVM acceleration is enabled:

- `kvmclock` - Paravirtualized clocksource (Linux v2.6.26+)
- `kvm-nopiodelay` - No PIO delay needed (Linux v2.6.26+)
- `kvm-asyncpf` - Async page faults (deprecated in Linux v5.10, use kvm-asyncpf-int)
- `kvm-steal-time` - Stolen time accounting (Linux v3.1+)
- `kvm-pv-eoi` - Paravirtualized EOI (Linux v3.10+)
- `kvmclock-stable-bit` - Trusted TSC for kvmclock (Linux v2.6.35+)

## Additional Features

| Feature | Description | Since |
|---|---|---|
| `kvm-pv-unhalt` | Paravirtualized spinlocks | Linux v3.12 |
| `kvm-pv-tlb-flush` | Paravirtualized TLB flush | Linux v4.16 |
| `kvm-pv-ipi` | Paravirtualized IPI | Linux v4.19 |
| `kvm-poll-control` | Host-side polling on HLT control | Linux v5.10 |
| `kvm-pv-sched-yield` | Paravirtualized sched yield | Linux v5.10 |
| `kvm-asyncpf-int` | Interrupt based async page faults | Linux v5.10 |
| `kvm-msi-ext-dest-id` | Extended Destination ID for interrupts | Linux v5.10 |

## Supplementary Features

- `kvm-pv-enforce-cpuid` - Limit supported paravirtualized features to exposed ones only
ENDOFFILE
