title: "nitro-enclave Virtual Machine"
source: "system/i386/nitro-enclave.rst"
---

# nitro-enclave Virtual Machine

`nitro-enclave` is a machine type which emulates an AWS Nitro Enclave virtual machine. It's based on `microvm` similar to how AWS nitro enclaves look like a Firecracker microvm.

Enclave VMs use vhost-vsock for communication with the parent EC2 instance and a Nitro Secure Module (NSM) device for cryptographic attestation. They boot from EIF (Enclave Image Format) files.

## Requirements

- `libcbor` and `gnutls` are required build dependencies
- vhost-device-vsock (or similar vhost-user-vsock backend) must run alongside

## Machine-Specific Options

- `nitro-enclave.vsock=string` (required) - chardev id for vhost-user-vsock
- `nitro-enclave.id=string` (optional) - Enclave identifier
- `nitro-enclave.parent-role=string` (optional) - Parent IAM role ARN
- `nitro-enclave.parent-id=string` (optional) - Parent instance identifier

## Running a nitro-enclave VM

Start vhost-device-vsock:
```
$ vhost-device-vsock \
   --vm guest-cid=4,forward-cid=1,forward-listen=9001+9002,socket=/tmp/vhost4.socket
```

Run the enclave VM:
```
$ qemu-system-x86_64 -M nitro-enclave,vsock=c,id=hello-world \
   -kernel hello-world.eif -nographic -m 4G --enable-kvm -cpu host \
   -chardev socket,id=c,path=/tmp/vhost4.socket
```

The parent instance VM always has CID 3. The enclave VM gets a dynamic CID (4 in this example). Applications connecting to the enclave should connect to CID 1 instead.
ENDOFFILE
