title: "Introduction"
source: "system/introduction.rst"
---

# Introduction

## Virtualisation Accelerators

QEMU's system emulation provides a virtual model of a machine (CPU, memory and emulated devices) to run a guest OS.

| Accelerator | Host OS | Host Architectures |
|---|---|---|
| KVM | Linux | Arm, MIPS, PPC, RISC-V, s390x, x86 |
| Xen | Linux (dom0) | Arm, x86 |
| MSHV | Linux (dom0) | x86 |
| Hypervisor Framework (hvf) | MacOS | x86, Arm |
| Windows Hypervisor Platform (whpx) | Windows | Arm, x86 |
| NetBSD VMM (nvmm) | NetBSD | x86 |
| Tiny Code Generator (tcg) | All | Arm, x86, Loongarch64, MIPS, PPC, s390x, Sparc64 |

## Feature Overview

- Wide range of device models including VirtIO devices
- vhost-user and Multi-process QEMU for device emulation offload
- Device passthrough support
- Full block layer with complex storage topology, snapshots and migration
- Flexible chardev system (stdio, files, unix sockets, TCP)
- Management interfaces: HMP (Human Monitor Protocol) and QMP (QEMU Monitor Protocol)
- GDB stub for debugging

## Running QEMU

General command line form:

```
$ qemu-system-xxx [machine opts] [cpu opts] [accelerator opts] [device opts] [backend opts] [interface opts] [boot opts]
```

Use `-M help` to list machine types. Use `help` as argument to any device option for parameter documentation.
ENDOFFILE
