# VM Templating

QEMU supports VM templating using shared memory backends, allowing multiple VMs to share common memory pages.

## Overview

VM templating allows you to:

1. Boot a template VM
2. Create additional VMs that share the template's memory
3. Each VM only uses memory for pages it modifies (copy-on-write)

This significantly reduces memory usage when running many similar VMs.

## Implementation

### Using memory-backend-file

Create a memory backend from a file:

```
qemu-system-x86_64 \
    -object memory-backend-file,id=mem0,size=1G,mem-path=/dev/hugepages/template \
    -machine memory-backend=mem0 \
    -kernel vmlinuz
```

### Template Creation

1. Boot the template VM normally
2. Pause and save memory to file
3. Subsequent VMs use the saved file as read-only memory backend

### Template Instantiation

```
qemu-system-x86_64 \
    -object memory-backend-file,id=mem0,size=1G,mem-path=/dev/hugepages/template,readonly=on \
    -machine memory-backend=mem0
```

## Considerations

- All VMs must use the same memory size
- Modified pages consume additional host memory
- Best suited for large deployments of identical VMs
- Works with KVM for hardware-assisted virtualization
