source: system/whpx.rst
---

# WHPX (Windows Hypervisor Platform)

QEMU accelerator using the Windows Hypervisor Platform (WHPX) API.

## Overview

WHPX is a hardware acceleration feature on Windows that allows QEMU to use the Windows Hypervisor Platform for virtualization. It is an alternative to WHPX (Windows Hypervisor Platform) and TCG.

## Requirements

- Windows 10 version 1803 or later
- Windows Hypervisor Platform feature enabled
- Intel VT-x or AMD-V hardware virtualization

## Enabling WHPX

1. Enable Windows Hypervisor Platform:
   - Control Panel > Programs > Turn Windows features on or off
   - Enable "Windows Hypervisor Platform"

2. Run QEMU with WHPX:
   ```
   qemu-system-x86_64 -accel whpx -m 2G -hda disk.qcow2
   ```

## Configuration

### CPU Configuration

WHPX supports:
- Multiple vCPUs
- CPU passthrough (host CPU features exposed to guest)
- Custom CPU models

### Memory

- Guest memory backed by host memory
- Large pages supported for better performance

## Limitations

- No nested virtualization support
- Limited migration capabilities
- Some features may not be fully implemented compared to KVM

## Troubleshooting

- Ensure Hyper-V is not conflicting (WHPX and Hyper-V are mutually exclusive on some Windows versions)
- Check Windows Event Log for WHPX errors
- Verify hardware virtualization is enabled in BIOS
